(6371414, 943889, 52336, '<td valign="top">Technically, this photo is sound -- great light (with caveats), perfect focus, nice post work.  Whereas the subject matter is "cute", it isn''t too much in my view.  I think where it is less successful is in the backdrop: a bold featureless stamp of black.  I can see that there is actually a dark drape of some kind that extends up the left quadrant of the frame, but it is generally lost in the clipping.  This backdrop is incongruous with your subject matter.  If it had been a white featureless backdrop, or some kind of relevant room, or a light backdrop with a prop or two nearby, the effect might have been less dramatic.  That''s the issue for me.  The backdrop says drama, the subject says cute.<br/> <br/>Another issue is the cramped composition.  Whereas the dog is nicely composed, it''s not the whole story.  Its bath is important, and the tub and bubble items are a tad cramped here.  Together with the dark backdrop, the composition becomes claustrophobic, and thus unappealing overall.<br/> <br/>One noticeable artifact is the shadow on the washboard.  I would have tried to diminish it with my light setup.<br/> <br/>Of story I can''t comment, because I don''t connect with subject matter like this generally.  Perhaps many people are in the same boat.  For me, content is as important as form, or at least my interpretation of it is, and I always like to feel something about the photographer in images.  I don''t get that experience here.</td>', 'Technically, this photo is sound -- great light (with caveats), perfect focus, nice post work.  Whereas the subject matter is "cute", it isn''t too much in my view.  I think where it is less successful is in the backdrop: a bold featureless stamp of black.  I can see that there is actually a dark drape of some kind that extends up the left quadrant of the frame, but it is generally lost in the clipping.  This backdrop is incongruous with your subject matter.  If it had been a white featureless backdrop, or some kind of relevant room, or a light backdrop with a prop or two nearby, the effect might have been less dramatic.  That''s the issue for me.  The backdrop says drama, the subject says cute. Another issue is the cramped composition.  Whereas the dog is nicely composed, it''s not the whole story.  Its bath is important, and the tub and bubble items are a tad cramped here.  Together with the dark backdrop, the composition becomes claustrophobic, and thus unappealing overall. One noticeable artifact is the shadow on the washboard.  I would have tried to diminish it with my light setup. Of story I can''t comment, because I don''t connect with subject matter like this generally.  Perhaps many people are in the same boat.  For me, content is as important as form, or at least my interpretation of it is, and I always like to feel something about the photographer in images.  I don''t get that experience here.', '2011-04-03 11:26:11.000000', NULL, 1),
(6371437, 943957, 100393, '<td valign="top">I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5.<br/> <br/>This is a nice flower image - I really like how well you''ve filled the frame but there are a few things that bothered me about it. I really like the round bokeh but that wide open lens has caused some chromatic aberration that was probably correctable, even if just through desaturation of cyan.<br/> <br/>I do enjoy the focus on the stem but that prominent blurred petal that is enface probably hurts the image a little - I think a quick pluck of that wouldn''t have gone amiss.<br/> <br/>Overall, a nice idea but for me, executed with a couple of niggles.</td>', 'I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5. This is a nice flower image - I really like how well you''ve filled the frame but there are a few things that bothered me about it. I really like the round bokeh but that wide open lens has caused some chromatic aberration that was probably correctable, even if just through desaturation of cyan. I do enjoy the focus on the stem but that prominent blurred petal that is enface probably hurts the image a little - I think a quick pluck of that wouldn''t have gone amiss. Overall, a nice idea but for me, executed with a couple of niggles.', '2011-04-03 11:57:34.000000', NULL, 1),
(6371440, 944037, 100393, '<td valign="top">I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5.<br/> <br/>A well executed image that offers up a slice of agricultural reality. However, for me, the good execution and the honestry of the scene don''t add up to something that holds my interest. The shot seems to be taken rather than chosen. Does that make sense?</td>', 'I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5. A well executed image that offers up a slice of agricultural reality. However, for me, the good execution and the honestry of the scene don''t add up to something that holds my interest. The shot seems to be taken rather than chosen. Does that make sense?', '2011-04-03 11:59:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(6371446, 943141, 100393, '<td valign="top">I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5.<br/> <br/>I imagine I''ll be off the curve here and my score of 5 will be lower than your average. I suppose that''s an admission that it is perhaps worth more. As a small niggle, the horizon isn''t horizontal - that always bothers me a little; but more than that, this sort of sunset scene always leaves me a bit cold. You''re right that twisty looking cloud is interesting and your processing serves it well but I''m someone who likes to see a bit more story in images. That isn''t to say I don''t score landscapes highly; often I do but they tend to be stunningly done with a very strong sense of depth - the flatness of the sea and the distant horizon doesn''t give you much of a chance to show perspective.</td>', 'I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5. I imagine I''ll be off the curve here and my score of 5 will be lower than your average. I suppose that''s an admission that it is perhaps worth more. As a small niggle, the horizon isn''t horizontal - that always bothers me a little; but more than that, this sort of sunset scene always leaves me a bit cold. You''re right that twisty looking cloud is interesting and your processing serves it well but I''m someone who likes to see a bit more story in images. That isn''t to say I don''t score landscapes highly; often I do but they tend to be stunningly done with a very strong sense of depth - the flatness of the sea and the distant horizon doesn''t give you much of a chance to show perspective.', '2011-04-03 12:11:24.000000', NULL, 1),
(6371447, 941002, 100393, '<td valign="top">Great landscape image but a hopeless entry at this size.</td>', 'Great landscape image but a hopeless entry at this size.', '2011-04-03 12:12:17.000000', NULL, 1),
(6371454, 943208, 100393, '<td valign="top">I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5.<br/> <br/>I like the idea behind this but the overall balance of the composition seems a bit off and the cropped hand and hair seem unnecessary. Also, I found the lighting is very flat. Investing in something like Viveza 2 would give you a bunch options to add a bit more depth to this.</td>', 'I am participating in the "Why a ''5'' not a ''6''?" commenting activity (see recent forum thread). Viewers are asked to leave a comment with some of their votes of ''5'' letting the photographer know why, for them, it fell short of a vote of ''6''. If you have found this comment useful, you may wish to offer some of your own to those images where you vote 5. I like the idea behind this but the overall balance of the composition seems a bit off and the cropped hand and hair seem unnecessary. Also, I found the lighting is very flat. Investing in something like Viveza 2 would give you a bunch options to add a bit more depth to this.', '2011-04-03 12:23:33.000000', NULL, 1),
